 "fn-23": "<a href=\"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/aijax/436/0/436_KJ00004077200/_pdf\"><code>santome-shinden-allotment</code></a> - 「The plot size of 5 chobu was the planning standard, but there is variation among the plots as completed. The size of the homestead, however, was an entirely equal allotment,」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「区画の規模5町歩は計画基準であるが，完成したものにはばらつきがある。しかし，屋敷の規模はまったくの均等配分であり」)「The development of Santome Shinden was all but complete in the spring of 1696 (Genroku 9), less than two years from the start of the work.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「三富新田の開発がほぼ完成するのは，着工から2年たらずの1696年（元禄9）春のことである。」) (The 5-cho plot size was the planning standard and the finished plots vary, but the homestead size was an entirely equal allotment; Santome's development was essentially complete in spring 1696 (Genroku 9), under two years from the start.)<!-- OCR renders まったく as またく. -->",
 "fn-24": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E8%8C%85%E8%91%BA\"><code>kayabuki-jawiki</code></a> - 「Basically the roof is made a steeply pitched one in order to prevent rain leaks, but」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「基本的に雨漏りを防止するために急勾配の屋根にするが」)「When the stems and the like of the plants used are thick, the gaps become larger and rain leaks come more easily, so a steeper pitch is required.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「使用する植物の茎などが太いと隙間が大きくなり雨漏りがしやすいため、より急勾配が要求される。」) (Basically the roof is made steep to prevent leaks; when the stems used are thick the gaps widen and leaks come easier, so a steeper pitch is required.)",
 "fn-25": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/屋敷林\"><code>yashikirin-jawiki</code></a> - 「On the west side and the south side, tall and sturdy trees, chiefly sugi, were planted in preparation against wind and snow. On the south side there were the storehouse (kura), the barn (naya) and the like, and fruit-bearing plants such as fig, grape and persimmon, and bamboo and the like, were planted.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「西側や南側には風や雪に備えて杉を中心に背の高く丈夫な木が植えられていた。南側には蔵や納屋などがあり、無花果や葡萄、柿などの果実がなる植物や竹などが植えられていた。」) (on the south were the storehouse and barn, with fruiting plants - fig, grape, persimmon - and bamboo.)",
 "fn-26": "<a href=\"https://www.city.sendai.jp/ryokuchihozen/kurashi/shizen/midori/hyakunen/documents/igunejyumokurist.pdf\"><code>sendai-igune-list</code></a> - 「[Tall trees] (height 15 m and over)」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「【高木】（高さ15m以上）」) with the rows 「sugi」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「スギ」)「kuromatsu」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「クロマツ」)「hinoki group」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「ヒノキ類」)「keyaki」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「ケヤキ」); 「[Sub-canopy and middle trees] (height around 10 m)」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「【亜高木・中木】（高さ10m前後）」); 「[Shrubs] (height around 3 m)」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「【低木】（高さ3m前後）」) (Tall trees (15 m and over): sugi, black pine, hinoki group, keyaki; sub-tall/middle trees (around 10 m); shrubs (around 3 m).)",
 "fn-27": "<a href=\"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/jass/38/2/38_37/_article/-char/en\"><code>minami-2022-igune</code></a> - 「10 m high Igune on the prevailing wind direction side」 (the abstract's own English); 「Looking at the aerial image in Fig. 1(1), the igune is placed on the west and north sides of the site so as to protect the living space from the strong west-northwest wind, the prevailing wind direction in winter.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「図 1(1)の空撮画像を見ると居久根は、冬期の卓越風向である西北西の強風から居住空間を守るように、敷地の西側及び北側に配置されている。」 - in the open-access full text at https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/jass/38/2/38_37/_pdf)",
 "fn-28": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/屋敷林\"><code>yashikirin-jawiki</code></a> - 「On the west side and the south side, tall and sturdy trees, chiefly sugi, were planted in preparation against wind and snow.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「西側や南側には風や雪に備えて杉を中心に背の高く丈夫な木が植えられていた。」) (jawiki); 「Tree heights run from 11 meters to 28 meters, averaging about 15 meters.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「樹高は１１メートルから２８メートルで平均１５メートルくらいである。」) (<a href=\"https://1073shoso.jp/www/sankyo/detail.jsp?id=18666\"><code>kashima-kainyo-1987</code></a>) (On the west and south tall sturdy trees, mainly sugi, were planted against wind and snow; tree heights 11-28 m, average about 15 m.)",
