 "fn-32": "<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1002/2475-8876.12146\"><code>ushijima-2020-manchu</code></a> - on the ways such a village has besides its main street, quoted by the paper from Guo Mo and others in the paper’s own English: 「the village streets in the northern plain region are extended to the east and west, and houses are closely lined up in the south and north sides of the street. ... There are two or three main streets in a village, which are extended to the east and west ... Roads extended to the north and south are supplemental. There are also small paths leading to farms, which are not often used」 (p. 349; the original’s 巷弄 is specifically alleys or lanes, and its 院院相连 says the dwellings along the street are courtyard joined to courtyard); and the access ways measured on the ground: 「the small paths (blind alleys) derive from Streets I to III for accessing residences are “Street IV (2-4 m wide)”」 (pp. 349-350). The ground BEHIND the lots is a different matter and a different date: 「After the late 1960s, farmers who lived in wide areas were gathered according to political measures, and the land behind the housing lots along the Old Road was developed so that the residential areas were expanded.」 (p. 355)<!-- read 2026-09-12 from the copy the GM downloaded. The paper does NOT support a traditional back lane; the footnote is re-aimed at what it does support -->",
 "fn-33": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Desire_path\"><code>desire-path-enwiki</code></a> - 「As few as 15 passages over a site can be enough to create a distinct trail, the existence of which then attracts further use.」 <!-- READ 2026-09-06, feature 195; the footnote formerly carried the registry summary -->",
 "fn-34": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Desire_path\"><code>desire-path-enwiki</code></a> - \"The path usually represents the shortest or the most easily navigated route through a natural area or greenspace.\" (the source's own English)",
 "fn-35": "<a href=\"https://99percentinvisible.org/article/least-resistance-desire-paths-can-lead-better-design/\"><code>ninety-nine-pi-desire</code></a> - \"Such unstructured routes can develop for all kinds of reasons. Some present obvious shortcuts or offer less-steep courses\" / \"Informal 'desire paths' can form with as few as fifteen traversals of an unpaved route, creating spontaneous new trails shaped by pedestrians effectively voting with their feet.\" (the source's own English)",
 "fn-36": "<a href=\"https://hig.diva-portal.org/smash/get/diva2:1773446/FULLTEXT01\"><code>ma-2024-desire-paths</code></a> - 「Hillier and Iida (2005) used a representation of street segment-based graphs and found a good correlation between the angular weighted ‘choice’ (betweenness centrality) and observed pedestrian movement in four areas of London, where pedestrians are believed to have angular preferences of fewest turns or least angle changes.」; The paper's own agent is given that preference as a rule rather than found to have it - it is one of the two guiding principles the model is built on, not a result: 「The global conception that is kept in mind in each agent is the aim to ‘Go straight!’ all along to its destination. This means that an agent has an OD plan from the beginning and continues facing straight towards its destination while searching for the next patch to step on. In this way, the agent can end up with a path with the least detoured distance from the straight line.」 (the version of record in the University of Gävle repository, open by the publisher's own hybrid license, where the paper's own site refuses every automated fetch; the turn preference is Hillier and Iida's finding as this paper reports it)",
 "fn-37": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Desire_path\"><code>desire-path-enwiki</code></a> - 「Hampton, Bruce; Cole, David (1988). Soft paths: how to enjoy the wilderness without harming it. Harrisburg, PA: Stackpole Books.」 <!-- READ 2026-09-06, feature 195; the footnote formerly carried the registry summary -->",
