 "fn-39": "<a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7538448/\"><code>pmc7538448-levee</code></a> - \"Farmers generally maintain levee grasslands by periodic mowing or herbicide application.\" (the source's own English)",
 "fn-40": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paddy_field\"><code>paddy-field-enwiki</code></a> - \"Some Mumun paddy fields in flat areas were made of a series of squares and rectangles, separated by bunds approximately 10 cm in height, while terraced paddy fields consisted of long irregular shapes that followed natural contours of the land at various levels.\" (the source's own English)",
 "fn-41": "<a href=\"http://www.knowledgebank.irri.org/step-by-step-production/pre-planting/land-preparation/how-to-construct-bunds\"><code>irri-bund-summary</code></a> - 「Construct no wider and taller than 50 cm x 30 cm bunds, around the field.」; 「Make sure that bunds are well compacted and properly sealed, with no cracks, holes, etc. This will minimize water losses through seepage (particularly in sloping lands).」 (the whole page is four lines; it serves its body to a browser and a Wayback capture of 2023 carries the identical text)",
 "fn-42": "<a href=\"https://www.fao.org/4/ac180e/AC180E07.htm\"><code>fao-ac180e-rice-fish</code></a> - \"the height of the dry bund should be at least 25 cm, but a height of 60 cm is recommended\" / \"Dykes (bunds) - Height: 60 cm; width at base: 180 cm width at top: 60 cm; slope - 45°\" (the source's own English)",
 "fn-43": "<a href=\"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/tga1948/25/3/25_3_145/_pdf/-char/ja\"><code>sugiura-1973-fuzoku</code></a> - 「The subject of the survey is the three hamlets of Asahi, Kita-Nagashida and Minami-Nagashida in Miyazaki-machi, Kami district, Miyagi prefecture.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「調査対象は, 宮城県加美郡宮崎町, 旭・北永志田・南永志田の 3 集落である」)「a farming district on a terrace surface that opens out to the south of the Tagawa, a tributary of the Naruse river」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「鳴瀬川の支流, 田川の南に開けた段丘面上の農村地帯」)「The survey was carried out in July 1972 over the whole of the 87 households of the subject hamlets. Anything that stands independent of the main house and has a roof was, provisionally, all regarded as an outbuilding」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「調査は, 1972 年7月に, 対象集落 87 戸全数につき行った。主屋と独立していて屋根を有するものは, 一応全て付属建物と見做し」) (The survey covered the three hamlets Asahi, Kita-Nagashida and Minami-Nagashida in Miyazaki-machi, Kami district, Miyagi; farmland on the terrace opened south of the Tagawa, a tributary of the Naruse; conducted July 1972 over all 87 households, counting every roofed structure independent of the main house as an outbuilding. (The hamlet name reads 永志田 Nagashida on the page.))",
 "fn-44": "<a href=\"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/tga1948/25/3/25_3_145/_pdf/-char/ja\"><code>sugiura-1973-fuzoku</code></a> - 「For the Tohoku region, which is generally said to be of the single-building type (Sato 1962), the subject hamlets have a surprisingly large number of outbuildings. The average number of outbuildings per household for the three hamlets combined is 4.38, and if the 10 non-farm households are excluded it becomes 4.57.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「対象集落は，一般に単棟式と言われる東北地方（佐藤1962）にしては，驚くほど付属建物が多い。3集落を合わせた1戸当り平均付属建物数は4.38であり，非農家10戸を除けば，4.57となる。」)",
 "fn-45": "<a href=\"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/tga1948/25/3/25_3_145/_pdf/-char/ja\"><code>sugiura-1973-fuzoku</code></a> - 「Table 5: by each function, according to main-house type / straw shed, hasa shed, compost shed, barn, work shed, livestock shed, coop, storehouse, firewood shed, pickle shed / 0.68, 0.13, 0.24, 0.58, 0.55, 0.32, 0.16, 0.24, 0.76, 0.24」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「第5表 主屋類型による各機能別 / わ は た な さ ち け く ま つ / 0.68 0.13 0.24 0.58 0.55 0.32 0.16 0.24 0.76 0.24」)header row 「straw / hasa / compost / barn / work / livestock / coop / storehouse / firewood / pickle」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「わ は た な さき ち け く ま つ」), B1 row 「0.68 0.13 0.24 0.58 0.55 0.32 0.16 0.24 0.76 0.24」 (Table 5's header columns run straw shed / hasa shed / compost shed / barn / work shed / livestock shed / coop / storehouse / firewood shed / pickle shed; the B1 (pre-1944) row reads 0.68, 0.13, 0.24, 0.58, 0.55, 0.32, 0.16, 0.24, 0.76, 0.24 in that order, so the livestock-shed (ち) column is 0.32.)<!-- OCR of a fax-scanned PDF. -->",
 "fn-46": "<a href=\"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/tga1948/25/3/25_3_145/_pdf/-char/ja\"><code>sugiura-1973-fuzoku</code></a> - 「Table 6: Number of houses in which each function is located inside the main house」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「第6表 主屋内各機能所在家数」) row 「Br 38 20(52.6) 12(31.6) 23(60.5) 7 4 2」「Figures in parentheses are percentages of the number of households.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「カッコ内は戸数に対する%」) (Table 6, functions located inside the main house: B1 houses 38, of which bath 20 (52.6%), privy 12 (31.6%), livestock 23 (60.5%); figures in parentheses are percentages of households.)",
 "fn-47": "<a href=\"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/tga1948/25/3/25_3_145/_pdf/-char/ja\"><code>sugiura-1973-fuzoku</code></a> - 「For example, at Sabusawa (寒風沢), some 5 km further back from the settlements under study, figures of 121 days of snow cover and a maximum snow depth of 145 cm are recorded (Showa 32 (1957), snow-cover survey, National Research Institute of Agriculture, Ministry of Agriculture and Forestry). / The settlements under study are, seen in terms of their location, comparatively isolated,」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「例えば，対象集落から5kmほど奥の寒風沢では，積雪日数121日，最高積雪深145cmという数字が記録されている（昭和32年，積雪調査，農林省農業総合研究所）。 / 本対象集落は，位置的に見て比較的隔絶し，」)",
 "fn-48": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/井戸\"><code>ido-jawiki</code></a> - 「Wells may be individually owned, and they may be jointly owned.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「井戸には個人所有の場合と共同所有の場合がある」)「In general, because the cost of digging a well was high, and because there were also many places where obtaining water was difficult from a topographical standpoint as well, jointly owned wells were numerous.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「一般には井戸の掘削費用が高額で地形的な観点からも水を得ることが困難な場所も多いことから共同所有の井戸が多かった」) (Wells were either privately or jointly owned; in general, because digging was costly and many places were topographically hard to get water in, jointly owned wells were common.)",
